WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.080
In the US, there are about 2,500 child
fatalities annually due to abuse or

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:14.040
neglect by a parent or caregiver. In
about half of those cases, child welfare

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.090
agencies were aware of the neglect and
abuse beforehand. How can these cases be

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:21.930
stopped from falling through the cracks?
Allegheny County, Pennsylvania, might hold

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.769
the key. They use predictive risk
modeling to target potentially dangerous

00:00:25.769 --> 00:00:30.029
family situations and help vulnerable
kids before it's too late. With

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:33.270
predictive risk modeling, different arms
of local governments like schools,

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.170
hospitals, and welfare agencies collect
routine information about the kids they

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.739
see. They share that information with
other agencies, and that information gets

00:00:40.739 --> 00:00:44.850
plugged into an algorithm and adds up to
a risk score. These scores help child

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:49.260
service agencies identify which cases
need to be investigated most urgently.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:52.949
This isn't about seizing children from
their parents. Removing kids from their

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.760
homes is a last resort. Importantly,
predictive risk modeling can help

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.480
determine which homes need assistance
from local agencies, and prevent abuse

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:03.780
and neglect before they happen.
If local governments are smart and

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.640
committed, they can use this technology
to save kids' lives.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.960
How do you think we can best prevent abuse and neglect among children?

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.680
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.120
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds,

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.610
and be sure to like and subscribe for
more research and videos from AEI.

